     "Phillip Cagan, then a young economist at the University of Chicago working with Milton Friedman, wrote *The Monetary Dynamics of Hyperinflation* in 1956, the first serious modern study of the subject. He needed a clean definition, so he picked one: an episode starts in the month prices rise more than 50 percent, and ends when monthly inflation falls below 50 percent and stays there for at least a year. The number is arbitrary, but it stuck because it captures a real change in behaviour. Below it, people still hold the money for a while. Above it, everyone tries to get rid of cash the moment it arrives, and that rush is what turns high inflation into collapse.",
