     "Rudolf Havenstein ran the Reichsbank from 1908 until his death on 20 November 1923, five days after the new Rentenmark appeared. He was a conscientious Prussian civil servant who saw his job as supplying the economy with the money it demanded. When prices rose, businesses needed more notes, so he printed more notes. In 1923 he spoke proudly to the Reichstag of how much new money his presses could now turn out. The British ambassador Lord D'Abernon wrote that no dog had ever chased its own tail with the speed of the Reichsbank. A 1915 caricature from the war-loan years already called him 'General Geldmarschall', the field marshal of money.",
