     "An ordinary 1,000-mark note with a big red stamp across it reading 'Eine Milliarde Mark'.",
     "Designing and printing new notes took weeks; prices doubled in days. So the Reichsbank took stocks of existing notes and stamped new values over them. This 1,000-mark Reichsbank note of December 1922 was overprinted in 1923 to read **one billion marks**, a millionfold promotion with a rubber stamp. Postage stamps went the same way, overprinted with Tausend, Millionen and Milliarden. By November 1923 the highest note was 100 trillion marks, printed on one side only to save time and ink.",
     "It captures the speed: there was no time even to design the money.",
     "A rubber stamp could make a note a million times richer, and the note still bought less.",
