     "The Rentenmark was a confidence trick in the best sense. Its legal backing was a mortgage charged on German agricultural and industrial land, and Rentenmarks could be swapped for interest-bearing bonds indexed to gold, but not for gold itself. What mattered was the quantity: just 500 million Rentenmarks were in circulation by 30 November 1923, 1.8 billion by July 1924. The old paper marks kept circulating alongside, and went on growing to 1.2 sextillion, but prices quoted in the new money stayed still. Twelve zeros were simply struck off price tags. Germans soon called the episode the 'miracle of the Rentenmark'.",
