     "Hungary's 1946 notes ran out of room for zeros, so they used words. 'B-pengo' meant billion-pengo, a million million. The **100 million b-pengo** note, 10 to the power 20 pengo, is the highest denomination ever issued for circulation. A note for a billion b-pengo, ten times larger, was printed but never issued. To keep taxes alive, the state created the **adopengo**, a tax unit whose value was announced daily by radio; by late July one adopengo was worth 2 sextillion pengo. When the forint arrived in August 1946, one forint replaced 400 octillion (4 followed by 29 zeros) pengo. The note shown here is that unissued one sextillion pengo note. Zimbabwe's 100 trillion note shows more zeros, but the pengo notes were far larger.",
