     "The breakup of Yugoslavia, war in Bosnia and UN sanctions from 1992 wrecked the economy of the rump Federal Republic of Yugoslavia (Serbia and Montenegro). The state printed to pay soldiers, pensions and loss-making firms. Monthly inflation was projected at a million percent by December 1993. Currency reforms struck off 1 zero, then 6, then 9. Notes went from thousands to millions to a 500 billion dinar note by the end of 1993. In January 1994 the central bank governor **Dragoslav Avramovic** launched a new dinar pegged one-to-one to the Deutsche Mark and backed by reserves. Inflation stopped almost at once, and Avramovic became a folk hero.",
